<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Urmia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Rezaiyeh</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Urmia is a city in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, the capital of West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> province, on a plain west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-urmia">Lake Urmia</a>. Long home to Muslims alongside Assyrian and Armenian Christians, it was a centre of Christian missions in the 19th century. After 1747 it was the seat of a khanate of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afshar-tribe">Afshar tribe</a>, absorbed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> Iran in the 1790s. It suffered severely during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/urmia.jpg" alt="Urmia" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ✿ Vlinder · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Uromia_Iran,pareeke_jangali_-_panoramio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Urmia lies at an elevation of 1,330 metres (4,360 ft) above sea level along the Shahar River on the Urmia Plain, near the borders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>. Lake Urmia, one of the world's largest salt lakes, lies to the east of the city and the Turkish border to the west. The city is the trading centre of a fertile agricultural region where apples, grapes and tobacco are grown.</p><p>Several derivations of the name have been proposed. Richard Nelson Frye suggested an Urartian origin, while T. Burrow connected it with the Indo-Iranian words urmi, wave, and urmya, undulating. It may also come from the Assyrian Aramaic words ur, city, and mia, water, meaning city of water. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-persian">Pahlavi</a> era (1925 to 1979) the city was called Rezaiyeh, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reza-shah">Reza Shah</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-minorsky">Vladimir Minorsky</a> there were villages on the Urmia Plain as early as 2000 BCE, under the influence of the Kingdom of Van. The city was an important town by the 9th century CE. After the Safavid shah Abbas the Great appointed Qasem Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afshar-tribe">Afshar</a> governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a> in 1622, an outbreak of plague forced him to leave, and he moved to western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, becoming the founder of the Afshar community of Urmia. The city became the capital of the Urmia Khanate in 1747, and the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> monarch, Agha Mohammad Khan, was crowned there in 1795. In the Safavid period the neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> made several incursions and captured the city more than once, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavids</a> regained control each time.</p><p>In 1835 Urmia became the site of the first American Christian mission in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, led by Justin Perkins with Asahel Grant. During the nineteenth century the region saw a short-lived Assyrian renaissance, with many books and newspapers published in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a>. Joseph Cochran and American medical colleagues built a medical college there in 1878. Around 1900 Christians made up more than 40 per cent of the city's population.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Urmia is the largest city of West Azerbaijan Province and the capital of the province and its county. Most residents are Azerbaijanis, with a large Kurdish minority and smaller Assyrian, Armenian and communities of speakers of Persian. Most are Muslims, but the city's churches, among them Assyrian, Armenian and Chaldean, preserve its Christian history, and it is the seat of a Chaldean Catholic archeparchy. Its population was 736,224 at the 2016 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. Urmia is known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> for volleyball and for Ashik music, which is registered in Iran's national heritage.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Urmia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They were in frequent conflict with the khans of Tabriz and <b>Urmia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/khanate-of-maragheh">Khanate of Maragheh</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Urmia</b> was home to a branch of the Turkic Afshar tribe, kin of Nader Shah.</blockquote><a href="/state/khanate-of-urmia">Khanate of Urmia</a></li></ul></section>
